          type: `welcome`,
          guideText: `{name}. We've spent this band on questions humans have asked for thousands of years. Today we turn to one that is brand new in the history of our species, and that your generation will face more directly than any before it: as technology gives us the power to change human beings themselves — our bodies, our minds, how long we live, maybe even whether we have to die at all — what should we do? This is the question of transhumanism, the idea of using technology to fundamentally enhance or even transform what it means to be human. It's not science fiction anymore, or not only: gene editing is real, brain-computer interfaces are being built, scientists work seriously on slowing aging, and people debate "designer babies" in actual policy rooms. So this is one of the genuinely important questions of your lifetime, and it's a philosophy question as much as a technology one — because the hard parts aren't "can we?" but "should we?", "who decides?", and "what might we gain or lose?". I'll handle it the way we handle everything: evenhandedly. There's a real case for excitement — curing terrible diseases, ending immense suffering, expanding human potential. There's a real case for caution — about dignity, unintended consequences, and what makes us human. And there's a crucial justice question — who gets access, and could this turn inequality into something written into biology itself? And I'll give you the protective tool you'll need most here: how to tell real science from the enormous hype and outright grift that already surrounds this, because people are ALREADY selling fake immortality. Let's think clearly about the future of being human.`,
