              audioPrompt: `Take the optimistic, pro-enhancement case seriously first, {name}, because it's genuinely powerful and comes from real compassion. The transhumanist argues: we already accept changing the human condition with technology, that's what all of medicine is, so the line between treating and enhancing is blurrier than people pretend. If we can prevent a child from being born with a devastating genetic disease, isn't that good? If we can extend healthy life so people get more years with those they love, isn't that good? If we can reduce suffering and expand capability, why refuse out of squeamishness? Much of what we now consider normal, vaccines, anesthesia, glasses, was once attacked as unnatural meddling. Human nature was never fixed or perfect; it's the rough product of blind evolution, full of cruelty, disease, and decay, and we have the power and arguably the responsibility to make it better. To refuse, they argue, is to choose suffering we could have prevented. Now take the cautious case just as seriously, because it captures real wisdom the optimist can miss. The careful thinker, sometimes called a bioconservative, doesn't oppose all change but warns: be very careful redesigning something as deep and little-understood as a human being. First, unintended consequences: biology is staggeringly complex, we understand it less than our confidence suggests, and changes, especially heritable ones passed to all future generations, could have effects we can't foresee or undo. Second, dignity and what we might lose: maybe some of our limits, even mortality, are bound up with meaning, motivation, humility, and the preciousness of finite life, so that fixing them could quietly destroy them. Third, the danger of hubris: the history of confidently improving complex systems we didn't understand is full of disasters. The cautious thinker isn't anti-progress; they're asking us to move slowly and humbly when the thing on the table is humanity itself. And there's a third consideration that cuts across both and may be the most urgent: justice and access. If powerful enhancements become real and expensive, as new tech usually is, who gets them? Enhancement could become something only the wealthy can afford, turning inequality into something far worse, a gap not just in money but in biology, with the rich becoming literally smarter, healthier, and longer-lived, possibly heritably. That could fracture humanity into enhanced and unenhanced classes. Notice these three considerations don't resolve into a simple yes or no. A wise view holds all of them at once: real openness to enhancements that reduce suffering, real caution about redesigning what we don't understand, and real insistence that the benefits not become a new tool of inequality. The honest position isn't a slogan; it's holding several real values in tension and thinking carefully, case by case.`,
